# Shared cache and conditional GET

The framework-owned `cache` addon is Redis for opt-in regions. It is not an automatic GUI page cache.

## Cache Leverage Check

Pause and decide **whether** expensive shared reads should use `cache` — do not
only ask how to wire Redis after the fact. Authority for how to cache correctly
stays in this rule plus `docs/sphinx/source/addon-cache.md`.

### When to run it

Run this check when **any** of the following is true:

- Creating a **new** addon (framework-owned or custom under `addons/<name>/`)
- Adding **substantial** new addon functionality (new catalogs, list/detail
  read paths, expensive shared aggregations, or heavy rework of those paths)

Skip it for trivial local edits: renames, typos, comments, one-line fixes,
CSS tweaks, or narrow bugfixes that do not introduce or rework expensive
shared reads. It is not a ceremony gate on every Direct tier change.

### Decision

Ask: will this addon (or this feature) repeatedly compute an **expensive,
stable, shared** value that many requests can reuse?

| Prefer caching | Do not cache in shared Redis |
| --- | --- |
| Shared catalogs and allowlists | Live per-viewer enrichment |
| Pure record / config snapshots invalidated on write | Pending/settle gate flags |
| Expensive shared aggregations with clear invalidation | High-churn job/run status tables |
| | Full GUI page JSON / `/api/pages` bodies |

When the answer is yes:

1. Prefer `optional_depends: ["cache"]` plus a deferred guarded bridge
   (`optional_namespace` or a small `utils/optional_cache.py`).
2. Fail open: when cache/Redis is unavailable, compute fresh and stay correct.
3. Invalidate or bump revision on writes that change list-visible data.
4. Escalate to hard `depends: ["cache"]` only when cache is intrinsically
   required (same modularity bar as other peers — see
   `framework-addon-structure.mdc`).

When the answer is no: leave the path uncached. Do not invent TTL wrappers for
volatile or per-viewer fields.

Discovery surfaces: `framework-addon-structure.mdc`,
`framework-addon-hygiene-checks.mdc`, `framework-addon-modularity`,
`docs/sphinx/source/first-addon.md`, and `docs/sphinx/source/addons.md`.

## What may be cached

- Shared cache may hold catalogs and pure shared snapshots invalidated on write — not live per-viewer enrichment, not in-flight pending/settle gate flags, not high-churn job/run status tables, and not full GUI page JSON.
- Do not introduce a full `/api/pages` Redis cache to “fix slow nav.” Fix payload build cost (batching, lean tiles) first; see `framework-components.mdc`.

## Volatile and per-viewer fields

- Fields that flip while background work runs (pending/settling gates) must be applied after a shared cache hit, not baked into the shared entry.
- If an ETag / If-None-Match validator is only a shared resource revision, per-viewer computed fields will stick stale on 304. Include a cheap viewer-state fingerprint in the validator, or skip conditional GET on viewer-driven refreshes.
- Clients polling while any row is still pending should not send If-None-Match against a revision that may have cached pre-settle state. Prefer `conditionalGetHeaders` from `pendingSettlePoll.js` so pending settle backups never poison themselves with a 304.

## Revision and invalidate races

- Short-circuit conditional GET before expensive serialization when the validator misses.
- Bump the shared revision whenever list-visible fields change (including per-viewer list fields that participate in ETag composition).
- A cache factory started before invalidate/bump must not write under the new revision; retry or discard when the revision changed mid-build.
- Counter / INCR keys and envelope-decoded gets must agree on value types. A decode path that always misses makes revision-based 304s lie.

## GET purity

- Cached or conditionally validated list GETs must be side-effect free. Tick, finalize, maintain, and mutate work belong on explicit write routes or scheduled jobs, with realtime deltas notifying clients (`page_paths=[]` when handlers apply in place).
